  "body": "Per @leaverou's [tweet](https://mobile.twitter.com/LeaVerou/status/1071848099088355328), `<iframe srcdoc>` currently automatically assumes its content is an HTML document. If you put in a string like `\"<svg>...</svg>\"`, it'll thus get autowrapped in html/body and not get the nice viewport relationship with the `iframe` that an SVG document would.\r\n\r\nUsing a `data:` url means the iframe is cross-origin now, so that's not useful either if you want to be able to poke at it. Right now the only reliable way to dynamically generate an SVG document in a iframe is to load a same-origin empty SVG over the network, then fill it via JS.\r\n\r\nCan we add or change something to allow using `srcdoc` for SVG documents? Either an autodetection based on the root element being `svg`, or a related attribute dictating what the document type is, or something like that?",
